From 4c18b78a26b62b18c5cb033f1ca7bbc1a69a3293 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yasuhiro Niji <ysniji@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 07:22:59 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] [de] Hang one Japanese character past the line end
 instead of pushing it out
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

When a character that may not begin a line (、。」 and the like) did not
fit at the end of a line, the line was broken at the previous break
opportunity, so the character before it moved to the next line too.
Word keeps the character at the end of the line, past the margin, and a
line laid out the other way ends one or two characters early. Forms and
documents written to a fixed number of characters per line then reflow
and run onto more lines and pages than in Word.

Now one East Asian character (U+3000 and above) that may not begin a
line stays at the end of the line. Only one hangs: if the line is
already past the margin, the break moves back as before. The width
pass records the hanging width, and the alignment leaves it out, so a
justified, centred or right-aligned line keeps its other characters in
place instead of squeezing the hanging one back inside the margin.

Assisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5
